Well, you can find a bit here...

http://www.stwing.upenn.edu/~avm/Amber.shtml

...but that's not much. The sword belonged to Corwin, the hero of the
first five Amber novels (much better, IMO, than the later ones) and
the father of Merlin (speaker of the quote you posted). His color was
silver and his symbol was a silver rose to match the blade. During a
couple of stretches in the series, he spent a lot of time slaying what
might be called demons, so that fits too. You can find a bit about him
here...

http://www.geocities.com/sword_and_mug/Corwin/

I don't really see the hallucination angle exactly, but one of the
devices of the series is about shadow worlds that imperfectly mimic the
real one. Corwin, belonging to the real world, might be said to be
immune to hallucinatory deceptions. But that seems like kind of a
stretch. Also, the series is all about the conflict between Order (Amber
and Corwin) and Chaos, so the alignment of the blade is reasonable
(though I wouldn't say that Corwin is perfectly lawful either). In
general, I don't remember Zelazny giving much of a background to the
blade itself, but again it's been a while.

If you haven't read at least the first five novels in the series, I'd
highly, highly recommend them. Absolutely top notch fantasy.

